========================================================================================
Introduction:
========================================================================================
Concurrent programs can go wrongs at 3 layers

<a> Atomicity: This is making sure that the sequence is protected from interference from other threads. This is the part most folks
worry about ( dont I sound like Jermey Manson now :) ) Locks and locks alone protect one from this.

<b> Visibility: Its making sure the effects (load/stores) in one thread are visible to other threads. This is where volatile/final/
synchronized come into play. 

<c> Ordering: Making sure there is a ordering between threads. This is again accomplished using final/volatile/synchronized.


========================================================================================
Basics:
========================================================================================
Problems with <b> and <c> arise because of:
<i>   Compiler level instruction reordering
<ii>  Processor level instruction reordering
<iii> Caching

bottomline, the sequence you write in your program is not how it appears on the wire. Though
the understanding is that from a single thread's perspective the ordering is done so as to make
it look-like-serial-execution ( aka keeping causality relationships in mind )


========================================================================================
Gory Details:
========================================================================================

The synchronization primitives are:

<Source A> Monitor Enter, volatile load, final load (within a constructor)

<Source B> Monitor Exit, volatile store, final store (within a constructor)


----> Reordering and synchronization primitives:
Normal load stores can be pushed ahead of <Source A> but never before. Keep lock coarsening in mind
and you ll remember. Again Source A and B cannot be interswapped.


----> Barriers and synchronization primitives:
Before and at Source A no barriers are involved but the first load/store ( whether volatile/normal/final ) after
Source A causes a load barrier ( aka make sure all prior loads have completed before issuing whatever the next
request is )


Before Store completes, the last load / store will be barriered meaning if its a load then a loadstore barrier
is used if its a store then a storestore barrier is used. The idea is to make sure prior transactions have 
completed.

Note: Before monitor enter ( not for volatile loads since they are usually kept in uncached memory ) the local
cachelines are invalidated as well.

========================================================================================
What other triggers these synchronization primiteves:
========================================================================================

<Source A> is trigerred by Thread.Run whereas Thread.Start invokes <Source B>

========================================================================================
Anything else one should be aware of:
========================================================================================

Yes final loads are treated as <Source A> and final stores are treated as <Source B>
when invoked in the constructor as you ve seen before. Please keep in mind non final fields
written during constructor are *NOT* treated as <Source A>!!